#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135013 :tutorial/content "**Periodic functions** are special functions that repeat themselves every $c$ units. The smallest positive value of $c$ that has this property is called the **period** of the function.\n\nLet's take the following periodic function as an example:\n\n![](images/f4/f483c51678bf15e97f1891d95ed9dda7e697ef18a593a507171a45e6cc7ffc7e.png)\n\nThis function repeats itself with a period of $2$. To illustrate, first focus on the part of the graph shown in blue below.\n\n![](images/fb/fbba2663aaca4514f0b6132ed36e48d7568637fe0faac327d6935b5f5df65541.png)\n\nIf we shift this part $2$ units to the right, the graph remains unchanged!\n\n![](images/de/deeadebca74eb6e466ebb13240e8f4cf9b0d2c03fedb5f8a94be18f1ddcd44f1.png)\n\nThe same would happen if we shifted the part $2$ units to the left.\n\nBy knowing the period of a periodic function, we can use a value of the function to infer many other values of the function. For example, in the function above, note that\n\n$$\nf(4)=0\n$$\n\nSince the period is $2$, if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $2$ from the input, the output stays the same. We can write this as\n\n$$\nf(4+2n) = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nFor example, setting\n\n$$\nn=1,2,3\\ldots\n$$\n\ngives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn = 1: f(4 + 2(1)) &= f(6) = 0 \\\\\nn = 2: f(4 + 2(2)) &= f(8) = 0 \\\\\nn = 3: f(4 + 2(3)) &= f(10) = 0 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe same is true for negative values of $n$. Setting\n\n$$\nn=-1,-2,-3\\ldots\n$$\n\ngives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn =-1: f(4 + 2(-1)) &= f(2) = 0 \\\\\nn =-2: f(4 + 2(-2)) &= f(0) = 0 \\\\\nn =-3: f(4 + 2(-3)) &= f(-2) = 0 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b8020c2e-cfe4-5187-94df-23d7d0fcb605" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1502 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
